  "partyLabels": "none-recorded",
  "note": "Proportional representation (single transferable vote) under the Local Government (Ireland) Act 1919: the first county council election in the five electoral areas that replaced the county electoral divisions of 1899, 21 seats. Polled Thursday 3 June 1920 (Belfast News-Letter, 3 June 1920, p. 6, \"To-day's Polling\"); counted at the Courthouse, Crumlin Road, Belfast, by Alexander Millar, secretary of the county council, as returning officer. Ballymena area (six seats, twelve candidates): 9,945 valid votes, quota 1,421; 17,773 electors. Counted on Saturday 5 June. The paper prints the first preferences of the six elected only. Crawford (1,731) and Wallace (1,508) were elected on the first count. Count 2, Crawford's surplus of 310: Kinnear to 1,446, elected. Counts 3 and 4, Wallace's surplus of 87 and Kinnear's of 25, elected no one. Count 5, the votes of O'Hara (printed \"537\" or \"337\", the first digit unclear), M'Kenna (160), M'Auley (103) and Mrs Gribben (105): Patrick from 1,201 to 1,290, Adrain from 924 to 1,001, M'Cann to 1,147 (the paper gives his first count as 1,003 and a gain of 140, which make 1,143). Count 6, Caruth's 949 votes: Patrick 283, to 1,573, elected. Count 7, Patrick's surplus of 152: Adrain to 1,467, elected; M'Cann, next with 1,176, was declared elected without the quota. No figure is printed for Clarke. The candidates' first names are from the list printed on polling day (Charles MacAuley there). The minute of the council's first meeting also records the chairmen of the rural district councils, who sat ex officio, and the co-opted members; they were not elected and are not listed here.",
  "sourceNote": "Reading uncertain (owner review, 12 November 2026): O'Hara's vote is printed \"537\" or \"337\" (the first digit is unclear); and M'Cann's printed first count (1,003) and fifth-count gain (140) make 1,143, against the printed total of 1,147, which is kept."
